course: dictionary
lesson: 8896
title: Shrivel: small, dry and wrinkled
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Shrivel means to become smaller, dry and wrinkled, often because of heat, cold or lack of water. Plants shrivel when they have no water, and grapes shrivel into raisins when they are dried.
explain: Look for clues in the words around it. «When its home dries out, a tardigrade pulls in its legs and head and shrivels into a tun.» Dries out is the clue: when something loses its water, it gets smaller and wrinkled.
explain: Shrivel can also describe a feeling. If you say «I shrivelled with embarrassment», you mean you felt very small and ashamed. Always try your meaning in the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: Shrivel is a verb. Its forms are shrivels, shrivelled (UK) and shriveled (US), and shrivelling (UK) and shriveling (US).
fact: «Shrivel» means to become smaller, dry and wrinkled.
fact: You can also shrivel something: heat can shrivel a leaf.
fact: Grapes shrivel into raisins when they are dried.
fact: In a story, «shrivel» can show someone feeling very small or ashamed.
exercise: «When its home dries out, a tardigrade pulls in its legs and head and shrivels into a tun.» What does shrivels mean here? | Grows bigger and softer | *Becomes small, dry and wrinkled | Swims away fast | Changes colour and glows
exercise: Dad says, «Don't leave the plant with no water, or its leaves will shrivel.» What will happen to the leaves? | They will turn into water | *They will become small, dry and wrinkled | They will shine brightly | They will grow taller
exercise: «I shrivelled with embarrassment.» What does shrivelled suggest? | She shivered with cold | She felt very big and proud | She laughed loudly | *She felt very small and ashamed
test: Which sentence uses shrivel to mean become small, dry and wrinkled? | The sun was bright and the sky was blue | *The apple shrivelled in the hot sun | The children shrivelled up with laughter at the party | The dog ran to fetch the ball
test: Which phrase means nearly the same as shrivel when it describes a feeling? | Grew bold and brave | Ran off to play outside | *Shrank inside with shame | Sang loudly to the crowd
exam: Which is the UK spelling of the past tense of shrivel? | shrivled | *shrivelled | shrivlled | shrivelt
